<!--
Rough initial documentation for Blockchain Tables.
This will need to be properly formatted and integrated into the PostgreSQL
documentation structure by a human developer.
-->

<chapter id="blockchain-tables">
 <title>Blockchain Tables</title>

 <sect1 id="blockchain-tables-intro">
  <title>Introduction</title>
  <para>
   Blockchain tables in PostgreSQL provide an append-only, immutable ledger-like
   functionality. They are designed for scenarios where data, once written,
   should not be altered or deleted, creating a verifiable history of events
   or transactions. This can be useful for audit trails, tracking historical
   changes, or any application requiring a high degree of data integrity and
   tamper-resistance at the database level.
  </para>
  <para>
   The core principle is that rows written to a blockchain table cannot be
   updated or deleted through standard DML commands.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="blockchain-tables-usage">
  <title>Usage</title>

  <sect2 id="blockchain-tables-creation">
   <title>Creating Blockchain Tables</title>
   <para>
    Blockchain tables are created using the <command>CREATE BLOCKCHAIN TABLE</command>
    syntax. This implicitly uses the "blockchain" access method.
   </para>
   <programlisting>
CREATE BLOCKCHAIN TABLE user_ledgers (
    event_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    user_id INT,
    event_payload JSONB,
    event_timestamp TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);
   </programlisting>
   <para>
    This command creates a new table named <literal>user_ledgers</literal> that will
    be managed by the blockchain access method. Standard column definitions,
    constraints (like PRIMARY KEY, NOT NULL, CHECK), and default values can be used.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="blockchain-tables-insert">
   <title>Inserting Data</title>
   <para>
    Data is inserted into blockchain tables using the standard <command>INSERT</command>
    command.
   </para>
   <programlisting>
INSERT INTO user_ledgers (user_id, event_payload) VALUES
    (101, '{"action": "login", "status": "success"}'),
    (102, '{"action": "profile_update", "field": "email"}');
   </programlisting>
   <para>
    Each successful insert represents a new "block" or entry in the immutable ledger.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="blockchain-tables-query">
   <title>Querying Data</title>
   <para>
    Blockchain tables can be queried using standard <command>SELECT</command> commands,
    just like regular tables. All standard SQL query capabilities, including
    joins, filtering, and ordering, are supported.
   </para>
   <programlisting>
SELECT * FROM user_ledgers WHERE user_id = 101 ORDER BY event_timestamp DESC;
   </programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="blockchain-tables-immutability">
   <title>Immutability: Restrictions on UPDATE and DELETE</title>
   <para>
    A key feature of blockchain tables is their immutability. Standard
    <command>UPDATE</command> and <command>DELETE</command> operations are
    not permitted on blockchain tables.
   </para>
   <programlisting>
-- These commands will fail:
UPDATE user_ledgers SET status = 'revoked' WHERE event_id = 1;
DELETE FROM user_ledgers WHERE user_id = 102;
   </programlisting>
   <para>
    Attempting such operations will result in an error, enforcing the
    append-only nature of the table.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="blockchain-tables-indexing">
   <title>Indexes</title>
   <para>
    Indexes (e.g., B-tree, GIN) can be created on blockchain tables to improve
    query performance, just like with regular tables.
   </para>
   <programlisting>
CREATE INDEX idx_user_ledgers_user_id ON user_ledgers (user_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_user_ledgers_payload_gin ON user_ledgers USING gin (event_payload);
   </programlisting>
  </sect2>

 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="blockchain-tables-limitations">
  <title>Current Limitations and Future Work</title>
  <para>
   This is an initial implementation of blockchain tables. Current limitations
   and areas for future development include:
  </para>
  <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        **Cryptographic Verification**: The current implementation enforces
        immutability at the SQL command level (preventing UPDATE/DELETE).
        Future work may involve adding cryptographic hashing of rows or blocks
        and linking them to provide stronger, verifiable tamper-evidence.
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        **TRUNCATE**: <command>TRUNCATE</command> behavior on blockchain tables
        needs to be defined and potentially restricted.
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        **ALTER TABLE**: Most <command>ALTER TABLE</command> operations that
        would modify existing row data or fundamental structure might be
        restricted. Adding new columns with default values or adding certain
        types of constraints might be permissible. This needs further refinement.
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        **MERGE Command**: The interaction of the <command>MERGE</command> command
        with blockchain tables, particularly its UPDATE and DELETE actions, needs
        to be fully specified and restricted to maintain immutability.
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        **Performance**: Performance characteristics, especially for very large
        append-only tables, will require ongoing analysis and optimization.
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        **Specialized AM Callbacks**: Many of the `TableAmRoutine` callbacks are
        currently basic implementations or stubs. Further development would
        involve tailoring these more specifically for blockchain table properties
        (e.g., `vacuum` could perform integrity checks).
      </para>
    </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
 </sect1>

</chapter>
